<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    MARS's bridge to pwiz-sharp, the .NET 10 port of the ProteoWizard core
    (ProteoWizard/pwiz PR #4619). It buys output formats MARS has no writer for - mzXML and
    mzMLb today - and puts the format code with the people who maintain the format.

    The reference is OPTIONAL and points at an external checkout, because pwiz-sharp has no
    package feed and is far too large to vendor the way third_party/Osprey.ML is. When the
    checkout is absent this project still compiles: MARS_NO_PWIZ drops the backend and
    PwizOutput reports itself unavailable, so a plain `dotnet build` with no pwiz anywhere
    keeps working and MARS keeps writing mzML through its own byte-splice writer. That is the
    same shape pwiz's own vendor projects use for their SDKs (NO_VENDOR_SUPPORT).

    Point it somewhere else with:

        dotnet build -p:PwizSharpDir=<path-to>/pwiz/pwiz-sharp
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>MARS.Pwiz</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>MARS.Pwiz</AssemblyName>
    <Description>Reads vendor formats and writes non-mzML formats, through pwiz-sharp.</Description>

  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="..\Pwiz.props" />

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PwizAvailable)' != 'true'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);MARS_NO_PWIZ</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    With pwiz present, audit this project's WHOLE package graph rather than only its direct
    references - which is the guard that keeps the vendor builds honest.

    Those builds turn off pwiz's own promotion of advisories to errors, because pwiz pins
    packages MARS cannot change. That would otherwise let any advisory against anything pwiz
    brings in ship without a word. Everything pwiz brings in reaches MARS through this project,
    so auditing its full graph checks the versions MARS actually resolves and ships - after
    any override below - and MARS's own projects still treat an advisory as an error. A
    vulnerable package that reaches the binary therefore fails the build, whichever project
    declared it.

    It is this, rather than listing vulnerable packages after the build, because that listing
    does not see a package that arrives through a project reference: with the override below
    removed, MARS resolved 8.0.4 and the listing still reported nothing. This restore audit
    does see it.

    Only with pwiz present. The plain build keeps "direct", for the reason given in
    Directory.Build.props.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PwizAvailable)' == 'true'">
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PwizSciexAvailable)' == 'true'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);MARS_SCIEX</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(PwizAvailable)' != 'true'">
    <Compile Remove="MarsSpectrumList.cs" />
    <Compile Remove="PwizWriteBackend.cs" />
    <Compile Remove="PwizSpectrumSource.cs" />
    <Compile Remove="VendorReaders.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(PwizAvailable)' == 'true'">
    <!--
      MsData alone. It carries the mzML, mzXML and mzMLb writers, so the writer path needs
      no vendor projects - which matters, because Analysis references the Waters reader and
      that stages a native Windows-only MassLynxRaw.dll into the output. Reading vendor RAW
      will need that chain; writing does not, and MARS ships four non-Windows artifacts.

      The wrapper therefore derives from SpectrumListBase (MsData) rather than
      SpectrumListWrapper (Analysis), which costs three delegating members.
    -->
<ProjectReference Include="$(PwizMsDataProject)" />

    <!--
      The Thermo reader, for reading .raw directly. This is the reference that costs
      something: Thermo.csproj pulls Analysis, which pulls the Waters reader, which stages a
      native Windows-only MassLynxRaw.dll into the output. Nothing in the Thermo path touches
      Waters, so it looks vestigial upstream - worth raising there rather than working around
      here. The Thermo SDK itself is managed and cross-platform.

      Vendor SDKs are license-gated: without -p:IAgreeToVendorLicenses=true the archive is not
      extracted and the reader compiles to something that recognizes a .raw but refuses to
      read it. MARS treats that the same as pwiz being absent.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(PwizThermoProject)"
                      Condition="Exists('$(PwizThermoProject)')" />

    <!-- Bruker: BAF, TSF and TDF including PASEF. Windows and Linux, from separate archives. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(PwizBrukerProject)"
                      Condition="Exists('$(PwizBrukerProject)')" />

    <!-- Sciex: .wiff through the legacy provider, .wiff2 through SCIEX.Apis.Data.v1. Windows
         only - see Pwiz.props. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(PwizSciexProject)"
                      Condition="'$(PwizSciexAvailable)' == 'true'" />

    <!--
      Sciex .wiff2 is a runtime-only plugin. Built as a dependency but deliberately NOT
      referenced: Wiff2LoadContext loads Pwiz.Vendor.Sciex.Wiff2.dll on demand into a
      side-by-side AssemblyLoadContext, so that its bundled SCIEX.Apis.Data.v1 does not collide
      with the signed Clearcore2 assemblies the legacy .wiff path uses in the default context.
      Referencing it normally would put it in deps.json and defeat that.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(PwizSciexWiff2Project)" Condition="'$(PwizSciexAvailable)' == 'true'">
      <ReferenceOutputAssembly>false</ReferenceOutputAssembly>
      <OutputItemType>Content</OutputItemType>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </ProjectReference>

    <ProjectReference Include="$(PwizSciexOfxStubProject)" Condition="'$(PwizSciexAvailable)' == 'true'">
      <ReferenceOutputAssembly>false</ReferenceOutputAssembly>
      <OutputItemType>Content</OutputItemType>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </ProjectReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Overrides the System.Security.Cryptography.Xml the Sciex reader brings in, so that what
    MARS ships is a patched release.

    pwiz's Sciex.csproj pins 8.0.4: a .NET 8-line version left over from before pwiz-sharp
    moved to .NET 10, whose retarget changed the target framework but not these explicit
    package versions. Four advisories were published against it on 2026-10-13 - CVE-2026-96958,
    96959, 96960 and 96222 - and the DLL goes into every Windows release of MARS, because those
    carry the Sciex reader.

    MARS cannot change pwiz's pin: the tree is cloned at the commit dotnet/pwiz-sharp.json
    names. It does decide what goes into its own binary. A direct reference here is nearer to
    MARS than pwiz's transitive one, so NuGet resolves this version for everything MARS builds
    and publishes. pwiz's own project still declares 8.0.4 and its restore still reports the
    advisories; the vendor builds stop that report from being fatal, and a check after the
    build fails if anything MARS actually ships has a known advisory - including this, should
    the override ever stop winning.

    10.0.x because MARS and pwiz-sharp are both net10.0 and that is the matching line. 8.0.5
    fixes the same advisories but would carry the stale pin forward. Remove this when pwiz
    moves its own pin to a patched 10.0.x release.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(PwizSciexAvailable)' == 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Xml" Version="10.0.14" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- MarsSpectrumList is internal, and its batching is the part most worth testing. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="MARS.Test" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MARS.Core\MARS.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MARS.IO\MARS.IO.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="ReportPwizAvailability" BeforeTargets="Build">
    <Message Condition="'$(PwizAvailable)' == 'true'"
             Importance="normal"
             Text="MARS.Pwiz: building against pwiz-sharp at $(PwizSharpDir)" />
    <Message Condition="'$(PwizAvailable)' != 'true'"
             Importance="normal"
             Text="MARS.Pwiz: no pwiz-sharp at $(PwizSharpDir); mzML output only. Set -p:PwizSharpDir to enable mzXML and mzMLb." />
  </Target>

</Project>
